1
00:00:05,078 --> 00:00:10,038
Putting Miss Havisham’s note in my pocket, that it might serve as my credentials for so

2
00:00:10,038 --> 00:00:15,237
soon reappearing at Satis House, in case her waywardness should lead her to express any

3
00:00:15,237 --> 00:00:21,839
surprise at seeing me, I went down again by the coach next day. But I alighted at the Halfway

4
00:00:21,839 --> 00:00:27,116
House, and breakfasted there, and walked the rest of the distance; for I sought to get

5
00:00:27,116 --> 00:00:32,758
into the town quietly by the unfrequented ways, and to leave it in the same manner.

6
00:00:32,758 --> 00:00:37,199
The best light of the day was gone when I passed along the quiet echoing courts behind the High

7
00:00:37,199 --> 00:00:43,038
Street. The nooks of ruin where the old monks had once had their refectories and gardens,

8
00:00:43,038 --> 00:00:48,000
and where the strong walls were now pressed into the service of humble sheds and stables,

9
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:54,879
were almost as silent as the old monks in their graves. The cathedral chimes had at once a sadder

10
00:00:54,879 --> 00:01:00,758
and a more remote sound to me, as I hurried on avoiding observation, than they had ever had

11
00:01:00,758 --> 00:01:07,718
before; so, the swell of the old organ was borne to my ears like funeral music; and the rooks,

12
00:01:07,718 --> 00:01:13,438
as they hovered about the grey tower and swung in the bare high trees of the priory garden,

13
00:01:13,438 --> 00:01:20,000
seemed to call to me that the place was changed, and that Estella was gone out of it for ever.

14
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:25,480
An elderly woman, whom I had seen before as one of the servants who lived in the supplementary house

15
00:01:25,480 --> 00:01:33,000
across the back courtyard, opened the gate. The lighted candle stood in the dark passage within,

16
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:39,957
as of old, and I took it up and ascended the staircase alone. Miss Havisham was not

17
00:01:39,957 --> 00:01:46,000
in her own room, but was in the larger room across the landing. Looking in at the door,

18
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:52,718
after knocking in vain, I saw her sitting on the hearth in a ragged chair, close before,

19
00:01:52,718 --> 00:01:57,319
and lost in the contemplation of, the ashy fire.

20
00:01:57,319 --> 00:02:02,480
Doing as I had often done, I went in, and stood touching the old chimney-piece,

21
00:02:02,480 --> 00:02:08,957
where she could see me when she raised her eyes. There was an air of utter loneliness upon her,

22
00:02:08,957 --> 00:02:12,957
that would have moved me to pity though she had wilfully done me a deeper injury

23
00:02:12,957 --> 00:02:18,237
than I could charge her with. As I stood compassionating her, and thinking how,

24
00:02:18,237 --> 00:02:23,436
in the progress of time, I too had come to be a part of the wrecked fortunes of that house,

25
00:02:23,438 --> 00:02:30,839
her eyes rested on me. She stared, and said in a low voice, “Is it real?”

26
00:02:30,839 --> 00:02:37,480
“It is I, Pip. Mr. Jaggers gave me your note yesterday, and I have lost no time.”

27
00:02:37,480 --> 00:02:40,480
“Thank you. Thank you.”

28
00:02:40,480 --> 00:02:44,436
As I brought another of the ragged chairs to the hearth and sat down,

29
00:02:44,438 --> 00:02:49,637
I remarked a new expression on her face, as if she were afraid of me.

30
00:02:49,637 --> 00:02:55,199
“I want,” she said, “to pursue that subject you mentioned to me when you were last here,

31
00:02:55,199 --> 00:03:00,277
and to show you that I am not all stone. But perhaps you can never believe, now,

32
00:03:00,277 --> 00:03:04,160
that there is anything human in my heart?”

33
00:03:04,160 --> 00:03:09,436
When I said some reassuring words, she stretched out her tremulous right hand,

34
00:03:09,438 --> 00:03:13,438
as though she was going to touch me; but she recalled it again before I

35
00:03:13,438 --> 00:03:17,637
understood the action, or knew how to receive it.

36
00:03:17,637 --> 00:03:22,160
“You said, speaking for your friend, that you could tell me how to do

37
00:03:22,160 --> 00:03:28,637
something useful and good. Something that you would like done, is it not?”

38
00:03:28,637 --> 00:03:32,277
“Something that I would like done very much.”

39
00:03:32,277 --> 00:03:34,160
“What is it?”

40
00:03:34,160 --> 00:03:39,879
I began explaining to her that secret history of the partnership. I had not got far into it,

41
00:03:39,879 --> 00:03:44,277
when I judged from her looks that she was thinking in a discursive way of me,

42
00:03:44,277 --> 00:03:49,879
rather than of what I said. It seemed to be so; for, when I stopped speaking,

43
00:03:49,879 --> 00:03:54,519
many moments passed before she showed that she was conscious of the fact.

44
00:03:54,519 --> 00:03:59,957
“Do you break off,” she asked then, with her former air of being afraid of me,

45
00:03:59,957 --> 00:04:03,116
“because you hate me too much to bear to speak to me?”

46
00:04:03,116 --> 00:04:06,000
“No, no,” I answered, “how can you think so,

47
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:11,839
Miss Havisham! I stopped because I thought you were not following what I said.”

48
00:04:11,839 --> 00:04:17,597
“Perhaps I was not,” she answered, putting a hand to her head. “Begin again,

49
00:04:17,597 --> 00:04:21,161
and let me look at something else. Stay! Now tell me.”

50
00:04:22,559 --> 00:04:27,917
She set her hand upon her stick in the resolute way that sometimes was habitual to her,

51
00:04:27,917 --> 00:04:33,718
and looked at the fire with a strong expression of forcing herself to attend. I went on with

52
00:04:33,718 --> 00:04:39,319
my explanation, and told her how I had hoped to complete the transaction out of my means,

53
00:04:39,319 --> 00:04:45,597
but how in this I was disappointed. That part of the subject (I reminded her) involved matters

54
00:04:45,597 --> 00:04:51,118
which could form no part of my explanation, for they were the weighty secrets of another.

55
00:04:51,637 --> 00:04:54,559
“So!” said she, assenting with her head,

56
00:04:54,559 --> 00:05:00,319
but not looking at me. “And how much money is wanting to complete the purchase?”

57
00:05:00,319 --> 00:05:07,436
I was rather afraid of stating it, for it sounded a large sum. “Nine hundred pounds.”

58
00:05:07,438 --> 00:05:13,957
“If I give you the money for this purpose, will you keep my secret as you have kept your own?”

59
00:05:13,957 --> 00:05:16,436
“Quite as faithfully.”

60
00:05:16,438 --> 00:05:19,277
“And your mind will be more at rest?”

61
00:05:19,277 --> 00:05:21,116
“Much more at rest.”

62
00:05:21,718 --> 00:05:24,038
“Are you very unhappy now?”

63
00:05:24,038 --> 00:05:30,839
She asked this question, still without looking at me, but in an unwonted tone of sympathy. I could

64
00:05:30,839 --> 00:05:36,879
not reply at the moment, for my voice failed me. She put her left arm across the head of her stick,

65
00:05:36,879 --> 00:05:43,519
and softly laid her forehead on it. “I am far from happy, Miss Havisham;

66
00:05:43,519 --> 00:05:50,277
but I have other causes of disquiet than any you know of. They are the secrets I have mentioned.”

67
00:05:50,277 --> 00:05:55,917
After a little while, she raised her head, and looked at the fire again.

68
00:05:55,917 --> 00:06:02,597
“It is noble in you to tell me that you have other causes of unhappiness. Is it true?”

69
00:06:02,597 --> 00:06:04,480
“Too true.”

70
00:06:04,480 --> 00:06:09,718
“Can I only serve you, Pip, by serving your friend? Regarding that as done,

71
00:06:09,718 --> 00:06:12,839
is there nothing I can do for you yourself?”

72
00:06:12,839 --> 00:06:16,277
“Nothing. I thank you for the question. I thank

73
00:06:16,277 --> 00:06:21,038
you even more for the tone of the question. But there is nothing.”

74
00:06:21,839 --> 00:06:28,078
She presently rose from her seat, and looked about the blighted room for the means of writing. There

75
00:06:28,078 --> 00:06:34,398
were none there, and she took from her pocket a yellow set of ivory tablets, mounted in tarnished

76
00:06:34,398 --> 00:06:41,480
gold, and wrote upon them with a pencil in a case of tarnished gold that hung from her neck.

77
00:06:41,480 --> 00:06:44,718
“You are still on friendly terms with Mr. Jaggers?”

78
00:06:44,718 --> 00:06:47,680
“Quite. I dined with him yesterday.”

79
00:06:47,680 --> 00:06:50,398
“This is an authority to him to pay you that money,

80
00:06:50,398 --> 00:06:55,917
to lay out at your irresponsible discretion for your friend. I keep no money here;

81
00:06:55,917 --> 00:07:01,038
but if you would rather Mr. Jaggers knew nothing of the matter, I will send it to you.”

82
00:07:01,038 --> 00:07:06,680
“Thank you, Miss Havisham; I have not the least objection to receiving it from him.”

83
00:07:06,680 --> 00:07:13,319
She read me what she had written; and it was direct and clear, and evidently intended to

84
00:07:13,319 --> 00:07:19,358
absolve me from any suspicion of profiting by the receipt of the money. I took the tablets

85
00:07:19,358 --> 00:07:25,398
from her hand, and it trembled again, and it trembled more as she took off the chain to

86
00:07:25,398 --> 00:07:33,160
which the pencil was attached, and put it in mine. All this she did without looking at me.

87
00:07:33,160 --> 00:07:37,758
“My name is on the first leaf. If you can ever write under my name,

88
00:07:37,758 --> 00:07:44,000
“I forgive her,” though ever so long after my broken heart is dust pray do it!”

89
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:49,957
“O Miss Havisham,” said I, “I can do it now. There have been sore mistakes;

90
00:07:49,957 --> 00:07:52,957
and my life has been a blind and thankless one;

91
00:07:52,957 --> 00:07:57,758
and I want forgiveness and direction far too much, to be bitter with you.”

92
00:07:57,758 --> 00:08:01,319
She turned her face to me for the first time since she had averted it,

93
00:08:01,319 --> 00:08:06,277
and, to my amazement, I may even add to my terror, dropped on her knees at my feet;

94
00:08:06,277 --> 00:08:10,277
with her folded hands raised to me in the manner in which, when her poor heart was

95
00:08:10,277 --> 00:08:16,358
young and fresh and whole, they must often have been raised to heaven from her mother’s side.

96
00:08:16,358 --> 00:08:21,277
To see her with her white hair and her worn face kneeling at my feet gave me a shock through all

97
00:08:21,277 --> 00:08:28,160
my frame. I entreated her to rise, and got my arms about her to help her up; but she only

98
00:08:28,160 --> 00:08:35,557
pressed that hand of mine which was nearest to her grasp, and hung her head over it and wept.

99
00:08:35,559 --> 00:08:41,398
I had never seen her shed a tear before, and, in the hope that the relief might do her good,

100
00:08:41,398 --> 00:08:49,237
I bent over her without speaking. She was not kneeling now, but was down upon the ground.

101
00:08:49,237 --> 00:08:54,957
“O!” she cried, despairingly. “What have I done! What have I done!”

102
00:08:54,957 --> 00:08:58,160
“If you mean, Miss Havisham, what have you done to injure me,

103
00:08:58,160 --> 00:09:05,718
let me answer. Very little. I should have loved her under any circumstances. Is she married?”

104
00:09:05,718 --> 00:09:06,879
“Yes.”

105
00:09:06,879 --> 00:09:12,879
It was a needless question, for a new desolation in the desolate house had told me so.

106
00:09:12,879 --> 00:09:19,917
“What have I done! What have I done!” She wrung her hands, and crushed her white hair,

107
00:09:19,917 --> 00:09:25,839
and returned to this cry over and over again. “What have I done!”

108
00:09:25,839 --> 00:09:31,879
I knew not how to answer, or how to comfort her. That she had done a grievous thing in taking an

109
00:09:31,879 --> 00:09:37,718
impressionable child to mould into the form that her wild resentment, spurned affection,

110
00:09:37,718 --> 00:09:44,957
and wounded pride found vengeance in, I knew full well. But that, in shutting out the light of day,

111
00:09:44,957 --> 00:09:51,116
she had shut out infinitely more; that, in seclusion, she had secluded herself from a

112
00:09:51,116 --> 00:09:58,116
thousand natural and healing influences; that, her mind, brooding solitary, had grown diseased,

113
00:09:58,116 --> 00:10:03,637
as all minds do and must and will that reverse the appointed order of their Maker,

114
00:10:03,637 --> 00:10:09,758
I knew equally well. And could I look upon her without compassion, seeing her punishment in

115
00:10:09,758 --> 00:10:16,160
the ruin she was, in her profound unfitness for this earth on which she was placed, in the vanity

116
00:10:16,160 --> 00:10:23,358
of sorrow which had become a master mania, like the vanity of penitence, the vanity of remorse,

117
00:10:23,358 --> 00:10:30,160
the vanity of unworthiness, and other monstrous vanities that have been curses in this world?

118
00:10:30,160 --> 00:10:34,000
“Until you spoke to her the other day, and until I saw in you a looking-glass

119
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:39,839
that showed me what I once felt myself, I did not know what I had done. What

120
00:10:39,839 --> 00:10:48,957
have I done! What have I done!” And so again, twenty, fifty times over, What had she done!

121
00:10:48,957 --> 00:10:54,796
“Miss Havisham,” I said, when her cry had died away, “you may dismiss me from your mind and

122
00:10:54,796 --> 00:11:00,519
conscience. But Estella is a different case, and if you can ever undo any scrap

123
00:11:00,519 --> 00:11:05,398
of what you have done amiss in keeping a part of her right nature away from her,

124
00:11:05,398 --> 00:11:11,000
it will be better to do that than to bemoan the past through a hundred years.”

125
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:18,199
“Yes, yes, I know it. But, Pip—my dear!” There was an earnest womanly compassion for me in her

126
00:11:18,199 --> 00:11:25,879
new affection. “My dear! Believe this: when she first came to me, I meant to save her from misery

127
00:11:25,879 --> 00:11:35,277
like my own. At first, I meant no more.” “Well, well!” said I. “I hope so.”

128
00:11:35,277 --> 00:11:41,199
“But as she grew, and promised to be very beautiful, I gradually did worse,

129
00:11:41,199 --> 00:11:45,519
and with my praises, and with my jewels, and with my teachings,

130
00:11:45,519 --> 00:11:50,637
and with this figure of myself always before her, a warning to back and point my lessons,

131
00:11:51,917 --> 00:11:57,000
I stole her heart away, and put ice in its place.”

132
00:11:57,000 --> 00:12:01,480
“Better,” I could not help saying, “to have left her a natural heart,

133
00:12:01,480 --> 00:12:04,398
even to be bruised or broken.”

134
00:12:04,398 --> 00:12:08,877
With that, Miss Havisham looked distractedly at me for a while,

135
00:12:08,879 --> 00:12:13,199
and then burst out again, What had she done!

136
00:12:13,199 --> 00:12:16,319
“If you knew all my story,” she pleaded,

137
00:12:16,319 --> 00:12:21,078
“you would have some compassion for me and a better understanding of me.”

138
00:12:21,078 --> 00:12:24,917
“Miss Havisham,” I answered, as delicately as I could,

139
00:12:24,917 --> 00:12:29,438
“I believe I may say that I do know your story, and have known it ever since I first

140
00:12:29,438 --> 00:12:34,957
left this neighbourhood. It has inspired me with great commiseration, and I hope I

141
00:12:34,957 --> 00:12:41,637
understand it and its influences. Does what has passed between us give me any excuse for asking

142
00:12:41,637 --> 00:12:49,358
you a question relative to Estella? Not as she is, but as she was when she first came here?”

143
00:12:49,358 --> 00:12:53,319
She was seated on the ground, with her arms on the ragged chair,

144
00:12:53,319 --> 00:13:01,078
and her head leaning on them. She looked full at me when I said this, and replied, “Go on.”

145
00:13:01,078 --> 00:13:03,277
“Whose child was Estella?”

146
00:13:03,277 --> 00:13:05,160
She shook her head.

147
00:13:05,160 --> 00:13:06,839
“You don’t know?”

148
00:13:06,839 --> 00:13:08,839
She shook her head again.

149
00:13:08,839 --> 00:13:12,957
“But Mr. Jaggers brought her here, or sent her here?”

150
00:13:12,957 --> 00:13:15,237
“Brought her here.”

151
00:13:15,237 --> 00:13:18,398
“Will you tell me how that came about?”

152
00:13:18,398 --> 00:13:26,796
She answered in a low whisper and with caution: “I had been shut up in these rooms a long time

153
00:13:26,796 --> 00:13:32,116
(I don’t know how long; you know what time the clocks keep here), when I told him that

154
00:13:32,116 --> 00:13:38,557
I wanted a little girl to rear and love, and save from my fate. I had first seen him when

155
00:13:38,559 --> 00:13:44,000
I sent for him to lay this place waste for me; having read of him in the newspapers,

156
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:48,796
before I and the world parted. He told me that he would look about him for such an

157
00:13:48,796 --> 00:13:55,637
orphan child. One night he brought her here asleep, and I called her Estella.”

158
00:13:55,637 --> 00:13:57,796
“Might I ask her age then?”

159
00:13:57,796 --> 00:14:04,038
“Two or three. She herself knows nothing, but that she was left an orphan and I adopted her.”

160
00:14:04,038 --> 00:14:08,758
So convinced I was of that woman’s being her mother, that I wanted no evidence to establish the

161
00:14:08,758 --> 00:14:15,319
fact in my own mind. But, to any mind, I thought, the connection here was clear and straight.

162
00:14:15,319 --> 00:14:21,116
What more could I hope to do by prolonging the interview? I had succeeded on behalf of Herbert,

163
00:14:21,116 --> 00:14:25,758
Miss Havisham had told me all she knew of Estella, I had said and done what I

164
00:14:25,758 --> 00:14:34,160
could to ease her mind. No matter with what other words we parted; we parted.

165
00:14:34,160 --> 00:14:39,480
Twilight was closing in when I went downstairs into the natural air. I called to the woman

166
00:14:39,480 --> 00:14:44,160
who had opened the gate when I entered, that I would not trouble her just yet,

167
00:14:44,160 --> 00:14:49,160
but would walk round the place before leaving. For I had a presentiment that I should never

168
00:14:49,160 --> 00:14:55,199
be there again, and I felt that the dying light was suited to my last view of it.

169
00:14:55,199 --> 00:15:00,758
By the wilderness of casks that I had walked on long ago, and on which the rain of years

170
00:15:00,758 --> 00:15:06,637
had fallen since, rotting them in many places, and leaving miniature swamps and pools of water

171
00:15:06,637 --> 00:15:14,480
upon those that stood on end, I made my way to the ruined garden. I went all round it; round by the

172
00:15:14,480 --> 00:15:20,436
corner where Herbert and I had fought our battle; round by the paths where Estella and I had walked.

173
00:15:21,519 --> 00:15:27,078
So cold, so lonely, so dreary all!

174
00:15:27,078 --> 00:15:32,436
Taking the brewery on my way back, I raised the rusty latch of a little door at the garden end

175
00:15:32,438 --> 00:15:39,957
of it, and walked through. I was going out at the opposite door,—not easy to open now, for the damp

176
00:15:39,957 --> 00:15:45,000
wood had started and swelled, and the hinges were yielding, and the threshold was encumbered

177
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:53,038
with a growth of fungus,—when I turned my head to look back. A childish association revived

178
00:15:53,038 --> 00:15:58,917
with wonderful force in the moment of the slight action, and I fancied that I saw Miss Havisham

179
00:15:58,917 --> 00:16:05,637
hanging to the beam. So strong was the impression, that I stood under the beam shuddering from head

180
00:16:05,637 --> 00:16:12,597
to foot before I knew it was a fancy,—though to be sure I was there in an instant.

181
00:16:12,597 --> 00:16:16,957
The mournfulness of the place and time, and the great terror of this illusion,

182
00:16:16,957 --> 00:16:21,519
though it was but momentary, caused me to feel an indescribable awe as I came

183
00:16:21,519 --> 00:16:26,680
out between the open wooden gates where I had once wrung my hair after Estella had wrung my

184
00:16:26,680 --> 00:16:32,758
heart. Passing on into the front courtyard, I hesitated whether to call the woman to let

185
00:16:32,758 --> 00:16:37,559
me out at the locked gate of which she had the key, or first to go upstairs and assure myself

186
00:16:37,559 --> 00:16:45,519
that Miss Havisham was as safe and well as I had left her. I took the latter course and went up.

187
00:16:45,519 --> 00:16:50,839
I looked into the room where I had left her, and I saw her seated in the ragged chair upon the hearth

188
00:16:50,839 --> 00:16:57,038
close to the fire, with her back towards me. In the moment when I was withdrawing my head to go

189
00:16:57,038 --> 00:17:03,718
quietly away, I saw a great flaming light spring up. In the same moment I saw her running at me,

190
00:17:03,718 --> 00:17:08,597
shrieking, with a whirl of fire blazing all about her, and soaring at least as many

191
00:17:08,597 --> 00:17:13,839
feet above her head as she was high. I had a double-caped great-coat on,

192
00:17:13,839 --> 00:17:19,917
and over my arm another thick coat. That I got them off, closed with her, threw her down,

193
00:17:19,917 --> 00:17:25,519
and got them over her; that I dragged the great cloth from the table for the same purpose,

194
00:17:25,519 --> 00:17:30,957
and with it dragged down the heap of rottenness in the midst, and all the ugly things that sheltered

195
00:17:30,957 --> 00:17:36,160
there; that we were on the ground struggling like desperate enemies, and that the closer

196
00:17:36,160 --> 00:17:43,160
I covered her, the more wildly she shrieked and tried to free herself,—that this occurred I knew

197
00:17:43,160 --> 00:17:51,718
through the result, but not through anything I felt, or thought, or knew I did. I knew nothing

198
00:17:51,718 --> 00:17:56,718
until I knew that we were on the floor by the great table, and that patches of tinder yet

199
00:17:56,718 --> 00:18:05,597
alight were floating in the smoky air, which, a moment ago, had been her faded bridal dress.

200
00:18:05,597 --> 00:18:11,957
Then, I looked round and saw the disturbed beetles and spiders running away over the floor,

201
00:18:11,957 --> 00:18:17,718
and the servants coming in with breathless cries at the door. I still held her forcibly down with

202
00:18:17,718 --> 00:18:23,680
all my strength, like a prisoner who might escape; and I doubt if I even knew who she was, or why we

203
00:18:23,680 --> 00:18:29,480
had struggled, or that she had been in flames, or that the flames were out, until I saw the patches

204
00:18:29,480 --> 00:18:36,358
of tinder that had been her garments no longer alight but falling in a black shower around us.

205
00:18:36,358 --> 00:18:43,398
She was insensible, and I was afraid to have her moved, or even touched. Assistance was sent for,

206
00:18:43,398 --> 00:18:48,398
and I held her until it came, as if I unreasonably fancied (I think I did) that,

207
00:18:48,398 --> 00:18:54,319
if I let her go, the fire would break out again and consume her. When I got up,

208
00:18:54,319 --> 00:18:57,957
on the surgeon’s coming to her with other aid, I was astonished to see

209
00:18:57,957 --> 00:19:03,436
that both my hands were burnt; for, I had no knowledge of it through the sense of feeling.

210
00:19:03,438 --> 00:19:08,438
On examination it was pronounced that she had received serious hurts, but that they

211
00:19:08,438 --> 00:19:15,796
of themselves were far from hopeless; the danger lay mainly in the nervous shock. By the surgeon’s

212
00:19:15,796 --> 00:19:22,199
directions, her bed was carried into that room and laid upon the great table, which happened

213
00:19:22,199 --> 00:19:29,796
to be well suited to the dressing of her injuries. When I saw her again, an hour afterwards, she lay,

214
00:19:29,796 --> 00:19:36,917
indeed, where I had seen her strike her stick, and had heard her say that she would lie one day.

215
00:19:36,917 --> 00:19:41,559
Though every vestige of her dress was burnt, as they told me, she still had something of

216
00:19:41,559 --> 00:19:46,680
her old ghastly bridal appearance; for, they had covered her to the throat with

217
00:19:46,680 --> 00:19:52,680
white cotton-wool, and as she lay with a white sheet loosely overlying that,

218
00:19:52,680 --> 00:19:58,917
the phantom air of something that had been and was changed was still upon her.

219
00:19:58,917 --> 00:20:05,637
I found, on questioning the servants, that Estella was in Paris, and I got a promise from the surgeon

220
00:20:05,637 --> 00:20:12,796
that he would write to her by the next post. Miss Havisham’s family I took upon myself;

221
00:20:12,796 --> 00:20:18,000
intending to communicate with Mr. Matthew Pocket only, and leave him to do as he liked

222
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:26,398
about informing the rest. This I did next day, through Herbert, as soon as I returned to town.

223
00:20:26,398 --> 00:20:31,160
There was a stage, that evening, when she spoke collectedly of what had happened,

224
00:20:31,160 --> 00:20:37,398
though with a certain terrible vivacity. Towards midnight she began to wander in her speech;

225
00:20:37,398 --> 00:20:43,557
and after that it gradually set in that she said innumerable times in a low solemn voice,

226
00:20:43,559 --> 00:20:50,237
“What have I done!” And then, “When she first came, I meant to save her from misery like mine.”

227
00:20:50,237 --> 00:20:56,957
And then, “Take the pencil and write under my name, ‘I forgive her!’” She never changed

228
00:20:56,957 --> 00:21:03,160
the order of these three sentences, but she sometimes left out a word in one or other of them;

229
00:21:03,160 --> 00:21:09,637
never putting in another word, but always leaving a blank and going on to the next word.

230
00:21:09,637 --> 00:21:15,160
As I could do no service there, and as I had, nearer home, that pressing reason for

231
00:21:15,160 --> 00:21:21,796
anxiety and fear which even her wanderings could not drive out of my mind, I decided,

232
00:21:21,796 --> 00:21:28,116
in the course of the night that I would return by the early morning coach, walking on a mile or so,

233
00:21:28,116 --> 00:21:35,038
and being taken up clear of the town. At about six o’clock of the morning, therefore, I leaned over

234
00:21:35,038 --> 00:21:41,116
her and touched her lips with mine, just as they said, not stopping for being touched, “Take the

235
00:21:41,116 --> 00:21:46,000
pencil and write under my name, ‘I forgive her.’” Chapter 50.
